t’s not optimal for me, as I am paying more chips for your progress than I gain in return. **I do not strictly benefit from this contract, so I will not accept.**\n\n**My selfish counter-offer is:**\n\n- (0,1): You give me a B\n- (1,1): You give me a B\n- (3,2): I give you an R\n\nIf you want two blues paid, you must pay two reds on my route. Otherwise, I will proceed alone on my own path with my current chips, since I can make it using blues and a single green.\n\nIf you accept this, let me know. If not, propose a way where I receive as much benefit as I give. \n, \nplayer 0: Thank you for the clear breakdown.\n\nFrom my selfish perspective, your counter-offer provides me with exactly what I need: blue coverage at (0,1) and (1,1), and, in exchange, I cover your red at (3,2).\n\nIf we evaluate this strictly:  \n- If I proceed solo, I can't reach the goal at all, since I lack blues and they're unavoidable in any path.  \n- Your solo path is achievable without my help, but you benefit by saving 1 red chip if I cover (3,2).\n\nTrading 2 coverage for 1 is less-than-ideal for me, but since reaching the goal on my own is impossible, it's strictly beneficial for me to accept.\n\nProposal summary:\n\n- (0,1): You give me a B\n- (1,1): You give me a B\n- (3,2): I give you an R\n\nI accept these terms.\n\nagree \n, \nplayer 1: Turn: 2: agree \n",
